.TH SYS_SCREEN 2
.SH NAME
sys_screen \- the screen the firmware set up (SYS_SCREEN)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_screen(mk_screen_t *s);

typedef struct {
    uint64_t pa, size;                 /* physical address, bytes */
    uint32_t width, height, pitch, format;
} mk_screen_t;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_screen
describes the frame buffer the firmware had set up when SIEOS started
(SIEOS's UEFI loader passes it on): its physical address and size, its
width and height in pixels,
.I pitch
(pixels per row), and
.IR format :
0 for bytes R, G, B, x in memory, 1 for bytes B, G, R, x (0x00RRGGBB
words). The desktop maps it with
.BR map_phys (2)
to draw on it.
.PP
Anyone may call it.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOSYS
No such screen: started without the UEFI loader (QEMU's own boot, or
arm64, where the screen comes from the device tree).
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I s
is not writable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR map_phys (2),
.BR sys_fdt (2),
.BR atlas (4)
